","Saltatory conduction is a type of nerve impulse transmission that is faster and more efficient than non-saltatory conduction. It occurs in nerve fibres called medulimentary fibres, which have a protective covering called the myic sheath. In contrast, non-saltatory conduction occurs in non-medulimentary fibres, which lack this sheath.

In saltatory conduction, the nerve impulse travels rapidly through the axon by jumping between the spaces between the myylic sheaths called nodes of Zoghian. This is because the myylic sheath acts as an insulator, allowing the electrical current to flow through the axon without significant resistance. The nerve impulse then "" ""propagates*’ to the next node, where it is recharged and moves again. This process is more efficient and faster than non-saltatory conduction, where the nerve impulse travels continuously along the entire axon.

In non-saltatory conduction, the nerve impulse travels continuously along the entire axon, with the electrical current reaching each point of the axon wall. This process is slower and less efficient because the electrical current encounters resistance along the entire length of the axon.

In summary, saltatory conduction is characterized by rapid, discontinuous nerve impulse transmission in medulimentary fibres, while non-saltatory conduction is characterized by continuous nerve impulse transmission in non-medulimentary fibres. Saltatory conduction is more efficient and faster due to the presence of the myylic sheath, which allows the nerve impulse to "" ""propagate*"" to the next node of Zoghian.
