","Stimulation of arterial baroreceptors leads to an increase in the activity of neurons in the nucleus tractritus solitulus (NTS). This occurs because arterial baroreceptors are distressible and respond to changes in blood pressure. When blood pressure increases, the receptors are stretched, causing them to release a greater number of acetylcholine molecules. This acetylcholine binds to nicotinic receptors on neurons in the NTS, stimulating them. The increased activity of these neurons in the NTS triggers a series of events that result in a decrease in blood pressure, such as relaxation of smooth muscle in the arterial smooth muscle and inhibition of sympathetic neurons. This negative feedback mechanism helps maintain blood pressure within a narrow range.
