","To determine the polarity of the carbon-oxygen bond in CO2, we need to look at the electronegativity values of carbon (C) and oxygen (O).

Electronegativity is a measure of an atom's ability to attract electrons in a chemical bond. The more electronegative atom will pull the electrons closer to itself, creating a partial negative charge (δ-) on that atom and a partial positive charge (δ+) on the less electronegative atom.

The electronegativity values for carbon and oxygen are:
- Carbon (C): 2.55
- Oxygen (O): 3.44

Since oxygen is more electronegative than carbon, it will pull the electrons in the carbon-oxygen bond closer to itself. This creates a polar bond with oxygen having a partial negative charge (δ-) and carbon having a partial positive charge (δ+).

However, CO2 has a linear molecular geometry with the two carbon-oxygen bonds being positioned 180 degrees apart. Due to this symmetry, the dipole moments of the two polar C-O bonds cancel each other out, resulting in a nonpolar CO2 molecule overall.
