"In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The key difference between σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals lies in the electron density distribution:  
- **σ molecular orbital**: The electron density is concentrated along the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This is a cylindrically symmetric distribution, meaning the electron density is the same along any plane passing through the internuclear axis.  
- **π molecular orbital**: The electron density is concentrated above and below the axis between the nuclei, forming two lobes. The electron density is zero along the internuclear axis.  

Thus, σ orbitals are formed by head-on overlap of atomic orbitals, while π orbitals are formed by side-by-side overlap.  

$\boxed{\text{σ orbital: electron density along the axis; π orbital: electron density above/below the axis}}$
