3. Aldehydes and Ketones: These compounds can lose a neutral molecule (e.g., HCN, HCNH, or CH3CN) or undergo cleavage at the carbonyl group, leading to the formation of a carbocation and a radical.

4. Amines: Amines can lose a proton (H+) to form a carbocation, or they can lose a neutral molecule (e.g., H2O or NH3) to form a carbocation.

5. Amides: Amides can undergo cleavage at the C-N bond, leading to the formation of a carbocation and an amine radical. They can also lose a neutral molecule (e.g., H2O or NH3) to form a carbocation.

