","Absolute asymmetric synthesis is a type of asymmetric synthesis where the reaction produces a product with a specific stereochemistry (e.g., R or S configuration) with 100% enantiomeric excess (ee). This means that only one enantiomer is formed, and no the other enantiomer is produced. In contrast, other types of asymmetric synthesis may produce a mixture of enantiomers, with varying levels of ee.

The key difference between absolute asymmetric synthesis and other types of asymmetric synthesis is the level of enantiomeric excess and the ability to predict the absolute configuration of the product. In asymmetric synthesis, the reaction conditions favor the formation of one enantiomer over the other, but the exact configuration (R or S) may not be known. In contrast, in absolute asymmetric synthesis, the absolute configuration of the product is known and guaranteed.

An example of asymmetric synthesis is the hydrogenation of a prochiral alkene using a chiral catalyst, such as a rhodium complex with a chiral ligand. This reaction can produce a mixture of enantiomers, with the ee depending on the catalyst and reaction conditions. In contrast, an example of absolute asymmetric synthesis would be the use of a chiral auxiliary or a chiral catalyst that can selectively produce one enantiomer with 100% ee.

For example, in the hydrogenation of (Z)-2-butene, a chiral rhodium complex with a chiral ligand can be used to produce either the R or S enantiomer of 2-butanol with high ee. However, if a chiral auxiliary is used to control the reaction, the absolute configuration of the product can be determined and guaranteed to be either R or S.

In summary, while all absolute asymmetric synthesis is asymmetric, not all asymmetric synthesis is absolute asymmetric. The key difference lies in the level of enantiomeric excess and the ability to predict the absolute configuration of the product.
