","1. **Compute the gradient vector \(\nabla z\) at the point \((4, 2, -2)\):**  
   The gradient vector \(\nabla z\) is normal to the surface at any point.  
   \[
   \nabla z = \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \left( \frac{56 - x^2 - y^2}{9} \right) \mathbf{i} + \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \left( \frac{56 - x^2 - y^2}{9} \right) \mathbf{j} + \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \left( \frac{56 - x^2 - y^2}{9} \right) \mathbf{k}
   \]
   \[
   = -\frac{2x}{9} \mathbf{i} - \frac{2y}{9} \mathbf{j} + \frac{2z}{3} \mathbf{k}
   \]
   At \((4, 2, -2)\):  
   \[
   \nabla z = -\frac{8}{9} \mathbf{i} - \frac{4}{9} \mathbf{j} - \frac{4}{3} \mathbf{k}
   \]

2. **Equation of the normal line:**  
   The equation of the line passing through \((4, 2, -2)\) and parallel to \(\nabla z\) is:  
   \[
   \begin{align*}
   x &= 4 - \frac{8}{9} t \\
   y &= 2 - \frac{4}{9} t \\
   z &= -2 - \frac{4}{3} t
   \end{align*}
   \]
