Prove that the Riemann-Liouville fractional derivative operators \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) do not commute in general, but they do commute if the sums involving the lower terminal conditions in the expressions for \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) \) and \( aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \) vanish. What are these conditions explicitly?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To prove the non-commutativity of the Riemann-Liouville fractional derivative operators \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \), we analyze the difference \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) - aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \). The expressions for these derivatives are as follows:

\[
aD_t^p f(t) = \frac{1}{a} \int_0^t t^{p-1} \frac{d^p}{dt^p} f(t) \, dt, \quad a > 0, \quad p > 0,
\]

\[
aD_t^q f(t) = \frac{1}{a} \int_0^t t^{q-1} \frac{d^q}{dt^q} f(t) \, dt, \quad a > 0, \quad q > 0.
\]

Substituting these into the difference \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) - aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \), we obtain:

\[
aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) - aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) = \frac{1}{a^2} \left[ \int_0^t t^{p-1} \frac{d^p}{dt^p} \left( \int_0^t t^{q-1} \frac{d^q}{dt^q} f(t) \, dt \right) \, dt - \int_0^t t^{q-1} \frac{d^q}{dt^q} \left( \int_0^t t^{p-1} \frac{d^p}{dt^p} f(t) \, dt \right) \, dt \right].
\]

The integrand in this expression is complex and typically does not simplify to zero. Thus, \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) \neq aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \) in general.

