","The recurrence relation for the number of comparisons in QuickScan in the best-case scenario is:
\[
C(n) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } n = 1, \\
C\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + C\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + n - 1 & \text{if } n > 1.
\end{cases}
\]
This recurrence is derived as follows:
1. **Base Case**: If the input list \( n \) has only one element, there are no comparisons, so \( C(1) = 0 \).
2. **Main Case**: For \( n > 1 \), the number of comparisons is derived as follows:
   - The first comparison is to compare the pivot with the first element, then the second element, and so on, until the pivot is placed in its final position. This takes \( n - 1 \) comparisons.
   - The remaining \( n - 1 \) elements are split into two equal-length sublots, each of size \( \lfloor n/2 \rfloor \) and \( \lceil n/2 \rceil \).
   - The number of comparisons for each sublot is given by the recurrence \( C(n/2) \).
   - Thus, the total number of comparisons is:
     \[
     C(n) = C\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + C\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + n - 1.
     \]
3. **Big-Order Analysis**: The time complexity of this recurrence is \( O(n \log n) \), which is the same as the time complexity of the Big-Order Analysis. 

The refined answer is:
\[
C(n) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } n = 1, \\
C\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \rfloor + C\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rceil + n - 1 & \text{if } n > 1.
\end{cases}
\]
