What is the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To determine the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a photon scattered by an electron, we use the Compton effect formula:  
\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2\pi \hbar}{m_e c} (1 - \cos \theta)
\]  
where:  
- \(\Delta \lambda\) is the change in wavelength,  
- \(\hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(1.054 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\)),  
- \(m_e\) is the electron mass (\(9.109 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\)),  
- \(c\) is the speed of light (\(3.00 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\)),  
- \(\theta\) is the scattering angle.  

The maximum wavelength change occurs when \(\theta = 180^\circ\) (i.e., the electron is scattered head-to-downwards). Substituting \(\theta = 180^\circ\) into the formula:  
\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{2\pi \hbar}{m_e c} (1 - \cos 180^\circ) = \frac{4\pi \hbar}{m_e c}
\]  

The percentage change in wavelength is:  
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}}}{\lambda_0} \right) \times 100\%
\]  
where \(\lambda_0 = 2.00 \, \text{Å} = 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m}\).  

Substituting the values:  
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{4\pi \times 1.054 \times 10^{-34}}{9.109 \times 10^{-31} \times 3.00 \times 10^8} \right) \times \frac{1}{2.00 \times 10^{-10}} \times 100\%
\]  
\[
\text{Percentage change} \approx 0.00035\%
\]  

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of the 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron is \(\boxed{0.00035\%}\).
