<start_of_turn>model
","(a) The proton is subject to a force \(F = qE\) due to the electric field, where \(q = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) and \(E = 500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
The net force is \(F = ma\), so the acceleration is:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{qE}{m} = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 3.99 \times 10^{11} \, \text{m/s}^2.
\]  
(b) The proton starts from rest (\(u = 0\)) and reaches a speed \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\).  
Using the kinematic equation \(v = u + at\), we solve for time \(t\):  
\[
t = \frac{v - u}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{3.99 \times 10^{11} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 8.25 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{s}.
\]  
Final answers:  
(a) The magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{3.99 \times 10^{11} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  
(b) The time required is \(\boxed{8.25 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{s}}\).
