"Calculate the value of normal gravity in mGal at a point P on the terrestrial ellipsoid at latitude \(60^\circ\)S and distance to the centre of 6362.121 km, given the Earth’s mass is \(5.9761 \times 10^{24}\) kg and the ratio between the polar and equatorial semi-axes is 0.9966. Use the first-order approximation.","<bos><start_of_turn>user
Calculate the value of normal gravity in mGal at a point P on the terrestrial ellipsoid at latitude \(60^\circ\)S and distance to the centre of 6362.121 km, given the Earth’s mass is \(5.9761 \times 10^{24}\) kg and the ratio between the polar and equatorial semi-axes is 0.9966. Use the first-order approximation.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the normal gravity \(g\) at point P, we use the first-order approximation formula:

\[
g = g_0 \left(1 + \frac{g_0}{g_0 - g_0 \cos^2 \theta}\right)
\]

where:
- \(g_0 = 9806.648 \, \text{m/s}^2\) (gravitational acceleration at the equator),
- \(\theta = 60^\circ\) (latitude),
- \(g_0 = 9806.648 \times \left(\frac{a}{r}\right)^2\) (gravitational acceleration at point P),
- \(a = 6357.8 \, \text{km}\) (mean equatorial radius),
- \(r = 6362.121 \, \text{km}\) (distance from point P to the centre).

First, calculate the gravitational acceleration at the equator \(g_0\):

\[
g_0 = 9806.648 \times \left(\frac{a}{r}\right)^2 = 9806.648 \times \left(\frac{6357.8}{6362.121}\right)^2 = 9806.648 \times 0.9985 = 9781.3 \, \text{m/s}^2
\]

Next, compute the Coriolis correction term:

\[
\frac{g_0}{g_0 - g_0 \cos^2 \theta} = \frac{9781.3}{9781.3 - 9781.3 \cos^2 60^\circ} = \frac{9781.3}{9781.3 - 9781.3 \times 0.25} = \frac{9781.3}{9781.3 \times 0.75} = 1.313
\]

Finally, compute the neutral gravity \(g\) at point P:

\[
g = g_0 \left(1 + \frac{g_0}{g_0 - g_0 \cos^2 \theta}\right) = 9781.3 \times (1 + 1.313) = 9781.3 \times 2.313 = 22,400 \, \text{m/s}^2
\]

Convert to mGal:

\[
g = 22,400 \times 9.81 \times 10^{-3} = 220 \, \text{mGal}
\]

Thus, the value of normal gravity at point P is \(\boxed{220 \, \text{mGal}}\).
