","To determine the reaction times that can be followed, we need to calculate the residence time of the solution in the flow tube. The residence time is the time it takes for a drop of solution to travel from the mixing chamber to the observation point (1–10 cm downstream).

1. **Calculate the cross-sectional area of the tubing**:
   \[
   A = \pi \left(\frac{d}{2}\right)^2 = \pi \left(\frac{0.1 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}}{2}\right)^2 = 3.14 \times 10^{-8} \text{ m}^2
   \]

2. **Calculate the linear flow velocity**:
   \[
   v = \frac{Q}{A} = \frac{10 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}^3/\text{min}}{3.14 \times 10^{-8} \text{ m}^2} = \frac{10 \times 10^{-6}}{3.14 \times 10^{-8}} \times \frac{1 \text{ min}}{60 \text{ s}} = 0.0528 \text{ m/s}
   \]

3. **Calculate the residence time for each distance downstream**:
   \[
   t = \frac{L}{v}
   \]
   For \( L = 1 \text{ cm} = 0.01 \text{ m} \):
   \[
   t = \frac{0.01 \text{ m}}{0.0528 \text{ m/s}} = 0.191 \text{ s}
   \]
   For \( L = 10 \text{ cm} = 0.1 \text{ m} \):
   \[
   t = \frac{0.1 \text{ m}}{0.0528 \text{ m/s}} = 1.88 \text{ s}
   \]

4. **Convert to seconds**:
   \[
   t = 0.191 \text{ s}, 1.88 \text{ s}
   \]

The reaction times that can be followed are \(\boxed{0.191 \text{ s}}\) to \(\boxed{1.88 \text{ s}}\) for the observation points 1 cm and 10 cm downstream, respectively.
