To find the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) in Cartesian coordinates, we use the following formula:

\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
V_x & V_y & V_z
\end{vmatrix}
\]

Given that \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), the \(z\)-component \(V_z = 0\). The curl simplifies to:

\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & 0 \\
V_x & V_y & 0
\end{vmatrix}
\]

Expanding the determinant:

\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(0) - 0 \right) \mathbf{i} - \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(0) - 0 \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(V_y) - \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(V_x) \right) \mathbf{k}
\]

Thus, the curl simplifies to:

\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]

Therefore, the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) that depends only on \(x\) and \(y\) is:

\[
\boxed{\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}}
